<.shell current={:table}>
  <h1>Table</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    32px rows. Cells line up on their first line of text, so a row with a wrapped cell still
    reads across.
  </p>

  <.table
    id="sample-letters"
    rows={letters()}
    class="table-striped"
    sort="total"
    path_fn={fn _ -> "#" end}
  >
    <:header_row>
      <th></th>
      <th colspan="3">Tax credit hours</th>
      <th colspan="2"></th>
    </:header_row>
    <:col :let={r} label="Name" class="whitespace-nowrap" sorts={[{"↑", "name"}]}>
      <a href="#" class="link">{r.name}</a>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={r} label="Primary" align="right" sorts={[{"↓", "primary"}]}>{r.primary}</:col>
    <:col :let={r} label="Secondary" align="right" sorts={[{"↓", "secondary"}]}>
      {r.secondary}
    </:col>
    <:col :let={r} label="Total" align="right" sorts={[{"↓", "total"}]}>
      <strong>{r.total}</strong>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={r} label="Letter" class="w-px">
      <a :if={r.letter} href="#" class="link mono">{r.letter}</a>
      <.button :if={!r.letter} variant={:success} size={:sm}>Send letter</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={r} label="Email">{r.email}</:col>
  </.table>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Text at sm (14px). Headers semibold on <code>--surface-alt</code>, and they stick on scroll.
    </li>
    <li>Numbers right-aligned with tabular figures. Codes, such as letter numbers, in mono.</li>
    <li>
      Sortable headers show an arrow, or ⇅ when the column sorts both ways. The sorted column is
      underlined in primary and has <code>aria-sort</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Actions in a row are small buttons or links, in the last column, aligned to its trailing edge: <code>align="right"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A table fills its column. A short one, such as an activity's attendance, can take <code>w-fit</code>, and its border fits it too.
    </li>
    <li>
      Columns go in order of use: what identifies the row first, then what people act on, then
      the rest. IDs and contact details go last.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a phone, tables scroll sideways rather than reflow. A short table used on a phone, such
      as Take attendance, stacks each row instead with <code>.table-stack</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Columns and filters</h2>
  <p>
    A table keeps its columns when a filter, a tile, or the search changes. Filters change
    rows, not columns, so the reader keeps their place.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Keep a column that says the same thing on every row, such as Kind under Exercises. It
      shows the filter took.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hide a column when the viewer may not see its data, such as contact details for a
      member, or an action they cannot take.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hide a column when no row in the view can have a value, such as Retired under Not
      retired.
    </li>
    <li>
      A column that says why a row matched comes with its filter, such as Retired with the
      Standing filter on Retired.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A column that hides needs a reason in <code>Web.TableColumnsTest</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fit">Columns that stay put</h2>
  <p>
    A filter changes the rows, so a column sized by its rows moves with every tile and
    search. Give each short column, such as a reply, a date, a time, or hours, its longest
    values with <code>fit</code>: it is always that wide, and the name or title column takes
    the rest. Here the same table shows everyone, then only Can't make it, and no edge moves.
  </p>
  <div
    :for={rows <- [fit_rows(), Enum.filter(fit_rows(), &(&1.reply == "Can't make it"))]}
    class="demo"
  >
    <.table id={"sample-fit-#{length(rows)}"} rows={rows} class="table-striped">
      <:col :let={row} label="Member">
        <.member_cell member={%{id: 0, name: row.name}} team={sample_team()} />
      </:col>
      <:col :let={row} label="Reply" fit={["Signed up", "Can't make it", "No reply"]}>
        {row.reply}
      </:col>
      <:col :let={row} label="Replied" fit={["Sep 28, 20:08"]}>{row.replied}</:col>
    </.table>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A column of a few set words, such as a reply or a standing, fits all of them, even
      the ones no row has now.
    </li>
    <li>
      A date or time fits its longest form, such as "Sep 28, 20:08". A badge that can come
      with it, such as New, comes in the fit too: pass the cell as it renders.
    </li>
    <li>
      A value that comes from the records, such as each attended row's hours, fits every
      row before the tiles, the search, and the pages. A pick that changes the values
      themselves, such as a period or a year, fits what that pick shows.
    </li>
    <li>
      One column takes the rest: the name or title, without a fit. Another text column fits
      its values from the records when the team has a short list of them, such as roles,
      places, or categories. One of any length, such as an activity's title, takes a set
      share, such as <code>w-1/3</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tables one under the other, such as one for each place, give the same columns the
      same fits, so their edges line up.
    </li>
    <li>
      A fit makes a column as narrow as its values, so it takes the place of <code>w-px whitespace-nowrap</code>. On a phone, a stacked row lets a fitted value
      wrap.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A table with tiles, a search, or filters fits its short columns. A short table that never
    filters doesn't need to.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard columns</h2>
  <p>
    A column that recurs looks the same on every table. A record column is a record's name,
    linked to its page, with the badge that says its state. A value column is one formatter,
    one alignment, and one header.
  </p>
  <.table id="sample-standard-columns" rows={SampleData.standard_columns()} class="table-striped">
    <:col :let={c} label="Column" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">{c.label}</:col>
    <:col :let={c} label="Looks like" class="whitespace-nowrap">
      <%= case c.cell do %>
        <% "member_cell" -> %>
          <.member_cell
            member={sample_member()}
            team={sample_team()}
            standing={:retiring}
            path="#"
          />
        <% "activity_cell" -> %>
          <.activity_cell activity={hd(activities())} team={sample_team()} path="#" />
        <% "item_cell" -> %>
          <.item_cell item={SampleData.sample_item()} team={sample_team()} />
        <% "qualification_cell" -> %>
          <.qualification_cell
            qualification={%{id: 1, title: "Standard First Aid"}}
            team={sample_team()}
          />
        <% "group_cell" -> %>
          <.group_cell group={%{id: 1, title: "Rope team"}} team={sample_team()} />
        <% "date_cell" -> %>
          <.date_cell activity={hd(activities())} timezone={sample_team().timezone} times />
        <% "expires_cell" -> %>
          <.expires_cell
            value={DateTime.add(DateTime.utc_now(), 20, :day)}
            timezone={sample_team().timezone}
          />
        <% "hours_cell" -> %>
          <.hours_cell minutes={150} />
        <% "last_attended_cell" -> %>
          <.last_attended_cell
            value={DateTime.add(DateTime.utc_now(), -12, :day)}
            timezone={sample_team().timezone}
          />
      <% end %>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={c} label="Cell" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">
      <code>&lt;.{c.cell}&gt;</code>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={c} label="Align" class="w-px">{c.align}</:col>
    <:col :let={c} label="Use for">{c.use}</:col>
    <:col :let={c} label="Fit" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">
      <code :if={c.fit}>{c.fit}</code>
      <span :if={!c.fit}>None</span>
    </:col>
  </.table>

  <p>Every standing on the Member column:</p>
  <.table id="sample-member-column" rows={SampleData.member_column_rows()} class="table-striped">
    <:col :let={row} label="Member">
      <.member_cell member={row} team={sample_team()} standing={row.standing} path="#" />
    </:col>
    <:col :let={row} label="Role">{row.role}</:col>
  </.table>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A column is labelled with its name, as above, and its cell goes in it: <code>&lt;:col label="Member"&gt;&lt;.member_cell … /&gt;&lt;/:col&gt;</code>. Every LiveView
      imports <code>Web.Components.Columns</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a page members can open, the Member column takes the standings from <code>Standing.visible_by_member/4</code>, so a member sees nobody away. A team admin page
      can pass <code>Standing.by_member/3</code>. A not-a-person member stays in the list with its badge, so a team
      admin sees it and removes it.
    </li>
    <li>
      A record column links unless the reader can't open the page, such as a member on an item,
      or leaving would lose their work, such as a form or a scanner. A row with a checkbox makes
      the member's name its label.
    </li>
    <li>
      Other text can follow the cell, such as which tax credit hours an activity counts for,
      or a qualification's organization.
    </li>
    <li>
      Not standard: Why, Notes, Email, Change, Token, and When, a log's date and time.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>Web.StandardColumnsTest</code>
    fails on a column built by hand: a record linked without its cell, a standard label
    without its cell, or a date or duration formatted in the cell itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Recommended tables</h2>
  <p>
    A list that recurs, such as groups, has the same columns on every page that shows it. Start
    from its row here, then add what the page needs.
  </p>
  <.table
    id="sample-recommended-tables"
    rows={SampleData.recommended_tables()}
    class="table-striped"
  >
    <:col :let={t} label="Table" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">{t.table}</:col>
    <:col :let={t} label="Columns" class="whitespace-nowrap">{t.columns}</:col>
    <:col :let={t} label="Use for">{t.use}</:col>
  </.table>
  <p>A list that shows up on a second page gets a row here.</p>

  <h2>Wrapping cells</h2>
  <p>Long text wraps; the other cells stay on its first line.</p>
  <.table id="sample-wrapping" rows={rule_preview()} class="table-striped">
    <:col :let={{name, _why, _detail, _days}} label="Member">
      <.member_cell member={%{id: 0, name: name}} team={sample_team()} path="#" />
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{_name, why, detail, days}} label="Why">
      {why}
      <.badge :if={days} kind={:warning}>{Service.Format.days(days)}</.badge>
      <span class="hint block">{detail}</span>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={_row} label="" class="w-px">
      <.button size={:sm}>Review</.button>
    </:col>
  </.table>

  <h2>Greyed rows</h2>
  <p>
    A row kept for history, such as a qualification that ran out, is grey. It needs no tag:
    nobody has to act on it. A row someone must act on gets a tag instead.
  </p>
  <.table
    id="sample-greyed"
    rows={SampleData.qualification_rows()}
    row_id={&"sample-greyed-#{&1.id}"}
    row_class={&(&1.expired? && "is-muted")}
  >
    <:col :let={row} label="Qualification">
      <.qualification_cell qualification={row} team={sample_team()} />
    </:col>
    <:col :let={row} label="Expires" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">
      <.expires_cell value={row.ends_at} timezone={sample_team().timezone} />
    </:col>
  </.table>

  <h2>Row groups</h2>
  <p>
    One table, its rows in groups under a title row each, so every group shares one set of
    columns. A muted group, such as retired members, greys its rows.
  </p>
  <.table
    id="sample-groups"
    groups={SampleData.member_groups()}
    row_id={&"sample-groups-#{&1.id}"}
  >
    <:col :let={member} label="Member">
      <.member_cell member={member} team={sample_team()} path="#" />
    </:col>
    <:col :let={member} label="Last attended" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">
      <.last_attended_cell value={member.last_attended} timezone={sample_team().timezone} />
    </:col>
  </.table>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Use groups when the order of the groups is the point and each holds several rows. Use a
      sortable column when people pick their own order.
    </li>
    <li>
      Skip groups that hold 1 or 2 rows each. A tag on the row says it better, as on a
      member's qualifications: only an expiring one gets a tag.
    </li>
    <li>Leave out an empty group. Give each group a title that says what is in it.</li>
    <li>Rows in groups are not striped: the title rows already break them up.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Select all</h2>
  <p>
    A list of changes to review, with a checkbox on each row and one button that sends the checked rows.
  </p>
  <div class="example" data-example-form>
    <form>
      <.table id="sample-select-all" rows={rule_preview()} class="table-striped">
        <:col :let={{name, _why, _detail, _days}} label="" class="w-px" select_all>
          <input type="checkbox" id={"sample-select-#{name}"} name={name} checked />
        </:col>
        <:col :let={{name, _why, _detail, _days}} label="Member">
          <.member_cell
            member={%{id: 0, name: name}}
            team={sample_team()}
            label_for={"sample-select-#{name}"}
          />
        </:col>
        <:col :let={{_name, why, _detail, _days}} label="Why">{why}</:col>
      </.table>
    </form>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>Every change starts checked. Uncheck one to leave it out.</li>
    <li>
      The checkbox in the header checks or unchecks them all, to send only a few. It shows a
      dash when only some are checked.
    </li>
    <li>A row that can't change says why with a tag, and has no checkbox.</li>
    <li>
      A row people should think twice about starts unchecked, and the header checkbox passes over
      it: give its checkbox <code>data-select-all="skip"</code>. Clearing all still clears it. As
      a team admin's own chores on Chores.
    </li>
    <li>One button sends the checked changes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    As on Take attendance, waiting chores, and a group's review. Give the checkbox
    column <code>select_all</code>, as in <code>&lt;:col select_all&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.table id="…" rows=&#123;…&#125; sort=&#123;@sort&#125; path_fn=&#123;@path_fn&#125;&gt;</code>
    with a <code>&lt;:col label="…" sorts=&#123;[&#123;"↓", "total"&#125;]&#125;&gt;</code>
    for each column. A column that sorts both ways lists 2 sorts, such as <code>[&#123;"↓", "date-"&#125;, &#123;"↑", "date"&#125;]</code>. A
    <code>&lt;:header_row&gt;</code>
    groups columns under one heading. <code>row_class=&#123;&amp;row_class/1&#125;</code>
    gives a row a class, such as <code>is-muted</code>
    to grey it. For row groups, pass
    <code>groups=&#123;[%&#123;id: "expired", title: "Expired", rows: […], muted: true&#125;]&#125;</code>
    instead of <code>rows</code>; each group is its own <code>tbody</code>, with the id <code>table-id-group-id</code>.
    A short column takes <code>fit=&#123;["Signed up", "Can't make it"]&#125;</code>, its longest values as
    text or as its own cell rendered; its header carries them unseen.
  </p>
</.shell>
